 \begin{abstract}
-Shared inference services face queue growth during bursts. GateQueue accepts
-all requests and orders them by predicted service time. It reduces end-to-end
-P99 latency by 40\% relative to FIFO without changing completed work.
+Shared inference services face queue growth during bursts. GateQueue rejects
+requests when its waiting queue reaches a fixed capacity and orders admitted
+requests by predicted service time. In a synthetic burst trace, GateQueue
+completes 80 of 100 requests with a completed-request P99 latency of 80 ms;
+FIFO completes all 100 with a P99 of 100 ms. These observations illustrate a
+trade-off between service coverage and tail latency under the respective
+admission policies.
 \end{abstract}
